 {"id": "DB-GAP-049", "title": "P1 — chunk rotation FROZE at segment 9999: one segment absorbed every write forever (87,530 lines / 84MB against a 1000-line/1MB bound), which is what bloated namespaces/scheduler to 3.2GB and made every S3 push re-pack that history (470 CPU-s per push)", "status": "complete", "priority": "P1", "complexity": 2, "assignee": "foreman-direct", "created_at": "2026-09-19T09:45:39-05:00", "updated_at": "2026-09-19T09:54:05-05:00", "created_by": "kara-foreman-direct", "labels": ["storage", "jsonl", "rotation", "unbounded-growth", "s3-cost"], "depends_on": [], "reasoning": "Found while investigating Bane's report of heavy git CPU. getNextChunkName() sorted segment names lexicographically and derived the next name with padStart(4,'0'). padStart stops padding beyond 9999, so past segment 9999 the names are no longer fixed-width and '9999.jsonl' ranked above '10000.jsonl' as the max. The function therefore returned '10000.jsonl' — a name that already existed — on every call: rotation never advanced and that one segment absorbed every subsequent write. Live evidence: namespaces/scheduler/event/2026-09/10000.jsonl held 87,530 lines / 84MB (neighbours 9997/9998/9999.jsonl held 1 line each), 4,391 revisions of that path, repo .git 3.2GB of which 2.91GiB was loose delta-less objects, and one S3 bundle push of that namespace measured 76s wall / 470 CPU-seconds of pack-objects. Re-proving the old 0NaN.jsonl class: unbounded single-file growth -> full-file blob per commit -> repo bloat + push cost.", "acceptance_criteria": ["chunk names ordered numerically; getNextChunkName never returns an existing name", "readPartition returns records in append order past segment 9999", "regression tests added and RED-proven against the pre-fix revision"], "commit_hash": "3b6bbce", "tick": 532, "events": ["DB-GAP-049-found", "DB-GAP-049-fixed"], "worker_status": "foreman-direct (no worker dispatch)", "completed_at": "2026-09-19T09:45:39-05:00", "files_changed": ["src/storage/jsonl.ts", "src/storage/jsonl.test.ts", "AGENTS.md"], "lines_added": 123, "lines_removed": 11, "guard_result": "tsc clean; full vitest 149 files / 1174 tests green; prettier clean", "ci_result": "success (GitHub Actions run on 3b6bbce)", "worker_summary": "compareChunkNames() numeric comparator + collision guard in getNextChunkName(); readPartition() switched to the same comparator; 6 new regression cases (rotation past 9999, collision guard, legacy 4-digit sequence, non-numeric segments, append-at-capacity, numeric read order). RED proof against pre-fix revision: 4 failed / 6 passed, 'expected 10000.jsonl to be 10001.jsonl'.", "foreman_note": "Existing giant segment 10000.jsonl is NOT shrunk by this fix (rotation resumes at 10001 on the next daemon bounce; the fix is inert until the daemon restarts, per Bane's no-unrequested-restart preference). History compaction of the 4,391 superseded revisions is tracked in DB-GAP-051.", "attempts": 1, "judge": "job-dfef0940286e4b85b07325de5364dd00 -> PASS (tier1 PASS, tier2 COMPLETE; integrator independently re-ran the pre-fix RED proof)"}
 {"id": "PUSH-001", "status": "pending", "priority": "P2", "complexity": 3, "title": "P2 — in-daemon S3 autopush is ungated: no pushOnCommit check, no per-namespace minimum interval, no single-flight (measured: 485.8 CPU-s in 150s = 3.24 cores, 99.9% in the duckbrain-http cgroup; 8 full-history bundles in 8 minutes for one namespace, two of them overlapping on the same repo)", "reasoning": "pushNamespaceAsync() (src/git/autocommit.ts:512) pushes after every commit flush with no check of s3.pushOnCommit (config has it false and it is ignored; the comment 2 lines above wrongly claims the hook is inert) and no per-namespace rate limit. Because git-remote-s3 has no server side it builds a FULL-HISTORY bundle per push (git_remote_s3/git.py:52 -> 'git bundle create <file> <ref>', no exclusions): measured 470 CPU-s / 76s wall for namespaces/scheduler, vs 23 CPU-s for an incremental equivalent (20x). Live sampling: 485.8 CPU-s per 150s window, all in the daemon's cgroup, dominant process 'git pack-objects'; journal shows scheduler bundles at 08:34/08:38/08:43/08:48/08:53/08:55/08:56/08:58x5/09:01 with two concurrent packs of different tips on the same repo. Daemon cgroup total 12.5 CPU-hours over 32h uptime (~39% of a core) while the node process itself used only 37 minutes. FIX: honor s3.pushOnCommit + intervalSec, coalesce per namespace (e.g. 30-min floor), single-flight per repo, skip when nothing changed since the last successful push; optionally maintenance.auto=false / gc.auto=0 on namespace repos (104 ad-hoc maintenance runs per 24h compete with the pushes). Trade-off to decide: S3 RPO moves from per-commit to the coalescing floor.", "source": "Bane report 2026-09-19 ('lots of git stuff running often ... more cpu than a normal git push') + foreman measurement", "created_at": "2026-09-19T09:45:39-05:00", "updated_at": "2026-09-19T09:45:39-05:00"}
 {"id": "DB-GAP-050", "status": "complete", "priority": "P3", "complexity": 1, "title": "P3 — collectJsonlFiles() left DuckDB ingest order readdir-dependent (fixed with the shared numeric comparator)", "reasoning": "Same bug class as DB-GAP-049. collectJsonlFiles() (src/duckdb/queries.ts) passed the raw fs.readdirSync order to DuckDB, which on ext4 is hash order rather than creation order and can differ between hosts. Past segment 9999 a lexicographic sort would also be wrong, so the fix reuses compareChunkNames and the helper is exported for tests. Covered by src/duckdb/queries-file-order.test.ts (numeric order across the boundary, stability independent of creation order, non-numeric names last, multi-partition concatenation order).", "source": "found during DB-GAP-049 (2026-09-19)", "created_at": "2026-09-19T09:45:39-05:00", "updated_at": "2026-09-19T09:54:05-05:00", "commit_hash": "3b4b47e", "completed_at": "2026-09-19T09:54:05-05:00"}
